Pushed a fix on top of your branch.

`ProtectedResourceMetadataController` built the document with `['resource' => $request->getSchemeAndHttpHost()] + $metadata`. The array union keeps the left operand, so the configured `resource` was always dropped and every firewall published the request origin. With two firewalls pinning `https://api.example.com/api` and `https://api.example.com/admin`, both `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/api` and `.../admin` answered `{"resource":"http://localhost"}`. RFC 9728, Section 3.3 makes such a document unusable: the value MUST be identical to the identifier the well-known suffix was inserted into. The existing test passed by coincidence, since its fixture's `resource` equals the request origin and the entry that carries a path is never requested. I added `testServesThePinnedResourceOfAFirewallCoveringAPath`, which fails on the old line.

Two points left for you:

The challenge is only emitted once an extractor found a token. `AccessTokenFactory` does not implement `EntryPointFactoryInterface`, so a request carrying no token gets a bare 401 with no `WWW-Authenticate` at all. That is the primary RFC 9728 discovery flow, and RFC 6750, Section 3 asks for the header there. The gap predates this PR, but the feature is about discovery, so it is worth either making the authenticator the entry point here or stating the limitation in the description.

The route loader only runs when the application imports it. An app that configures `resource_metadata` and forgets the import gets a challenge advertising a URL that 404s, with no diagnostic. `symfony/recipes` already ships `_security_logout` and `_security_oidc_login`; this needs the same block.

Smaller ones: `parse_url()` returning `false` makes the validator accept the value, so a malformed `resource` is published as is; the invalid-identifier message cites Section 2, where the identifier is defined in Section 1.2, which also makes "no query" a SHOULD NOT rather than a MUST; and the document ships escaped slashes, where every other discovery document does not.

