by nicolas-grekas at 2026-09-08T06:46:40Z

@alexandre-daubois is right, and I pushed the first of the two fixes he names.

The path of the identifier is read when the route is declared, so a value the container cannot parse leaves the route at the bare well-known path while the document publishes an identifier carrying a path. A client then derives `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/api`, gets a 404, and even if it found the document RFC 9728 §3.3 would make it refuse the answer, since the `resource` it names is not the identifier the URL was derived from. Resolving in the controller cannot fix it: the route is static, so the mismatch would remain.

So a `resource` that parses as no URL at all is now refused at build time rather than silently mis-served. What still works, and is the answer for anyone driving this from the environment, is interpolating the variable inside the URL:

```yaml
resource: 'https://%env(API_HOST)%/v1'
```

There the scheme and the host parse, the path stays readable, and the route and the document agree. The test that asserted the old behaviour moved from the valid provider to the invalid one, and the message points at the working form.

The one thing this gives up is pinning the whole identifier from an env var when it carries no path. That case is still served by leaving `resource` out, which resolves the origin per request. If you would rather keep the placeholder accepted and document the restriction instead, say so and I will put it back.

